## Original Content
Investors will buy your life insurance policy today — and collect the payout when you die.

## Summary
The article discusses how the AIDS epidemic led to the development of a multibillion-dollar financial market where investors purchase life insurance policies from individuals and collect the payout upon their death. This practice, known as life settlements or death speculation, highlights the intersection of public health crises and financial speculation.

## Key Points
- The AIDS crisis was a key driver in creating a large death-speculation market.
- Investors buy life insurance policies upfront from policyholders.
- The payout from these policies is collected by investors when the insured person dies.
- This market has grown to be worth multibillions of dollars.
- It involves financial speculation on mortality, raising ethical and social implications.


